BrowserX is published as **source-available** software: you may read the source
and run the browser for yourself, and nothing more. This licence is deliberately
restrictive — it is not an open-source licence, it is not approved by the OSI,
and it grants no rights beyond the ones written below.

---

## 1. Definitions

**"The Software"** means the BrowserX source code, build scripts, documentation,
assets and any binary built from them, in whole or in part.

**"The Author"** means codero-sus, the copyright holder.

**"Personal use"** means use by an individual, on devices they own or control,
for their own private, non-commercial purposes.

---

## 2. What you MAY do

Provided you comply with section 3, you may:

1. **Read** the source code and learn from it.
2. **Build** the Software and **run** it for your own personal use.
3. **Keep personal copies**, including backups, on your own devices.
4. **Report bugs**, request features and suggest improvements to the Author.

That is the complete list. Everything else is prohibited unless the Author gives
you written permission (section 4).

---

## 3. What you MAY NOT do

1. **No commercial use.** You may not use the Software, or anything built from
   it, in connection with any commercial activity. This includes (without
   limitation): use by or on behalf of a company, employer or organisation; any
   paid, ad-supported, subscription or monetised product or service; client or
   contract work; internal business tooling; resale, rental or paid support; and
   bundling the Software with anything that is sold.

2. **No modification.** You may not modify, adapt, translate, patch, extend,
   refactor, rebrand, repackage or otherwise create derivative works from the
   Software. You may not remove, obscure or alter any copyright, credit, licence
   or attribution notice.

3. **No redistribution.** You may not publish, upload, post, mirror, share,
   sublicense, sell, rent, lease, bundle or otherwise distribute the Software —
   source code or binaries — in whole or in part, through any channel, including
   public repositories, package registries, app stores, file-sharing sites, or
   as part of another product. Publishing a link to the official repository is
   allowed; copying its contents is not.

4. **No circumvention, no transfer.** You may not work around these restrictions,
   and you may not sublicense, assign or transfer any of the limited rights in
   section 2 to anyone else. Third parties have no rights under this licence.

